CLUSTER-and-ELLIPTICAL rows (sigma >= 250 km/s): a single relic must be at least m = 14.68 eV to supply the densest of them
  GALAXY-and-DWARF rows (sigma < 100 km/s): the LIGHTEST mass any of them could be supplied by is m = 93.26 eV
  [PASS] A1 (THE CONDITIONAL WORKS, and it is the sharpest thing in this session) the framework's own residuals predict their own explanation: a single fermion mass supplies every cluster, group and elliptical row and is FORBIDDEN by phase space in every galaxy and dwarf row.  The two requirements do not overlap -- the clusters need at least a few eV and the dwarfs would need hundreds   (clusters need m >= 14.68 eV; dwarfs would need m >= 93.3 eV, a factor 6 higher.  A relic at the cluster mass CANNOT be dense enough in a dwarf, which is exactly the observed pattern)

====================================================================================================================
2. does that mass also give the right cosmological abundance?
====================================================================================================================
  a thermally produced light fermion has Omega h^2 = m/(94 eV) for one degree of freedom (Lesgourgues & Pastor).
    m =  14.68 eV -> Omega_nu h^2 = 0.1561 vs Omega_dm h^2 = 0.1200 (ratio 1.301); needs 0.77 degrees of freedom or non-thermal production
    m =   5.00 eV -> Omega_nu h^2 = 0.0532 vs Omega_dm h^2 = 0.1200 (ratio 0.443); needs 2.26 degrees of freedom or non-thermal production
    m =   8.00 eV -> Omega_nu h^2 = 0.0851 vs Omega_dm h^2 = 0.1200 (ratio 0.709); needs 1.41 degrees of freedom or non-thermal production
    m =  11.00 eV -> Omega_nu h^2 = 0.1170 vs Omega_dm h^2 = 0.1200 (ratio 0.975); needs 1.03 degrees of freedom or non-thermal production
    m =  15.00 eV -> Omega_nu h^2 = 0.1596 vs Omega_dm h^2 = 0.1200 (ratio 1.330); needs 0.75 degrees of freedom or non-thermal production
  [PASS] A2 the abundance NEARLY closes but lands just below the window, and that is a real cost: a single thermal degree of freedom gives the full dark-matter density at 11.3 eV, while the densest cluster row needs at least 14.7 eV -- so the conditional requires 0.77 effective degrees of freedom, i.e. mild non-thermality or a degeneracy factor below 2, rather than closing for free   (thermal closure at m = 11.3 eV against a cluster floor of 14.68 eV: the ratio is 0.77, so g_eff = 1.54 rather than 2.  The window 14.7-93 eV is real but the abundance does not fall in it for free)
  ⚠️ THE KNOWN KILL, and it is this programme's own: a THERMAL relic of ~11 eV is a hot species that contributes
  Delta N_eff ~ 1 and is excluded by the CMB (ballistic_survivor_window_2026.py already found and recorded this).
  The escape is NON-THERMAL production (resonant, a la Shi-Fuller), which decouples the abundance from N_eff -- that
  is Angus's own route and it is NOT tested here.  So this conditional is coherent but NOT established.

====================================================================================================================
3. the radial test, which is where this conditional can be falsified on data already in hand
====================================================================================================================
  a phase-space-limited relic is DENSITY-CAPPED, so it must fill a cluster from the OUTSIDE IN and saturate in the
  core.  The liability table's radial run is the opposite: the residual GROWS inward (X-COP 1.48 -> 2.09 -> 2.76 -> 2.91
  from 0.9 R500 to the core, and R2500 far worse than R500 in the groups).  Test whether the cap is even reached.
  X-COP clusters 0.9 R500          rho_need 1.418e-24  rho_max(11.3 eV, sigma=1000) 1.770e-20  need/cap = 0.0001
  X-COP clusters 0.5 R500          rho_need 1.630e-23  rho_max(11.3 eV, sigma=1000) 1.770e-20  need/cap = 0.0009
  X-COP clusters 0.2 R500          rho_need 2.155e-22  rho_max(11.3 eV, sigma=1000) 1.770e-20  need/cap = 0.0122
  X-COP cores 30-100 kpc           rho_need 2.197e-21  rho_max(11.3 eV, sigma=900) 1.291e-20  need/cap = 0.1703
  CLASH 14-600 kpc                 rho_need 4.405e-22  rho_max(11.3 eV, sigma=1100) 2.356e-20  need/cap = 0.0187
  Bullet BCG1 300 kpc              rho_need 2.275e-22  rho_max(11.3 eV, sigma=1200) 3.059e-20  need/cap = 0.0074
  X-ray groups R500                rho_need 4.949e-25  rho_max(11.3 eV, sigma=400) 1.133e-21  need/cap = 0.0004
  X-ray groups R2500               rho_need 1.733e-23  rho_max(11.3 eV, sigma=400) 1.133e-21  need/cap = 0.0153
  eRASS1 groups 1e12.5-13.5        rho_need 2.988e-24  rho_max(11.3 eV, sigma=300) 4.780e-22  need/cap = 0.0063
  X-ray ellipticals 5-70 kpc       rho_need 3.750e-22  rho_max(11.3 eV, sigma=250) 2.766e-22  need/cap = 1.3556
  SLUGGS GC systems logM*>11.3     rho_need 7.927e-22  rho_max(11.3 eV, sigma=250) 2.766e-22  need/cap = 2.8658
  [PASS] A3 AGAINST THE CONDITIONAL, and it relocates the boundary: at the thermal-abundance mass the phase-space cap is EXCEEDED in the X-ray ellipticals (need/cap 1.36) and the SLUGGS globular-cluster systems (2.87), so a single relic cannot supply those rows at all.  The clean split is therefore NOT clusters-versus-dwarfs but sigma >~ 300 km/s versus everything below, and two of the liability rows fall on the forbidden side of it   (exceeds the cap: X-ray ellipticals 5-70 kpc at 1.36; SLUGGS GC systems logM*>11.3 at 2.87; every genuine cluster/group row sits at need/cap <= 0.170)
  [PASS] A3b and the same numbers say phase space does NOT explain the cluster PROFILE: in every true cluster row the cap is unsaturated by one to four orders of magnitude, so a density-capped relic would fill clusters smoothly, while the measured residual GROWS inward (1.48 -> 2.09 -> 2.76 -> 2.91 from 0.9 R500 to the core)   (cluster/group rows span need/cap = 0.0001 to 0.1703; the cap only bites where sigma <= 250 km/s)

====================================================================================================================
4. mutation controls
====================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 the Tremaine-Gunn formula is right: a 1 eV fermion at sigma = 1000 km/s must allow a far higher density than the same fermion at sigma = 1 km/s, by the cube of the ratio   (ratio = 1.000e+09 against the analytic 1e9)
  [PASS] M2 mutation: with boost = 1 (no residual) every required density is zero and no relic is needed   (boost = 1 gives rho_need = 0 identically)

====================================================================================================================
VERDICT, in conditional mode
====================================================================================================================
  ASSUMING the framework is right in discs, its own failures DO predict their own explanation, and the prediction is
  sharper than I expected.  A single light fermion supplies every genuine cluster and group residual and is FORBIDDEN
  BY PHASE SPACE in every dwarf and galaxy row: the clusters need m >= 14.7 eV, the dwarfs would need >= 93 eV, and
  those two requirements are a factor 6 apart with nothing fitted.  The framework's own 28-row liability table selects
  that story without being asked to.  Credit where it is due: this is Angus's hot-dark-matter MOND cosmology
  (Angus 2009; Angus, Famaey & Diaferio 2010), and this programme's own ballistic-survivor script had already found
  the 5-11 eV window.  What is new is that the liability table picks it out unprompted.
  THREE COSTS, all reported against the conditional.  (i) The abundance does not close for free: a thermal degree of
  freedom gives the full dark-matter density at 11.3 eV, just BELOW the 14.7 eV cluster floor, so the story needs
  g_eff ~ 1.5 or mild non-thermality.  (ii) The boundary is not where I said: the X-ray ellipticals and the SLUGGS
  globular-cluster systems EXCEED the cap (need/cap 1.36 and 2.87), so the clean split is sigma >~ 300 km/s versus
  everything below, and two liability rows sit on the forbidden side.  (iii) The radial profile is wrong: the cap is
  unsaturated by one to four orders of magnitude in every true cluster row, so a capped relic fills clusters smoothly
  while the measured residual GROWS INWARD.  Phase space explains the galaxy ABSENCE beautifully and the cluster
  PROFILE not at all.  And the known N_eff kill on a thermal 11 eV relic still stands unless production is non-thermal.
  NET: this is the best conditional story available and it is coherent on the amount and on the galaxy/cluster split,
  and it fails on the radial profile, on two intermediate-sigma rows, and on N_eff.  That is a far more specific place
  to stand than 'clusters do not work', and every one of those three failures is a computable next test.

